# Environment files

Read this before running the applications and their tests. Developers write the environment files themselves. `mise run setup` neither creates, changes nor deletes them, and they never get into the clones' git.

## Per clone

Each clone documents its own environment files, usually in its README or an example file such as `.env.example`. Here, per clone, is which file the application reads when it runs from the meta-repository, where its example is, and which values differ locally. `mise run repos:sync` generates the table below from the `env_file`, `env_example` and `env_notes` fields in `repos.toml`; `/bootstrap` or a developer fills them in. Explanations that do not fit a table cell go under a heading per clone, outside the markers.

<!-- repos:begin:env -->

| Clone | File | Example | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |

<!-- repos:end:env -->

A clone without an example file gets an entry in [../clone-backlog.md](../clone-backlog.md).

## Tools from mise

Commands from the root run with the tools in the root `mise.toml`, Node 24 among them. A clone with its own `mise.toml` is trusted by `mise run setup` and uses its own versions inside its folder. A clone's git hooks may look for tools elsewhere (for example nvm); a commit from a terminal with mise activated works, a commit from a graphical git client may not.
